Patch 30: stop zeroing 128 KB of palette map on every mode search.

Bit-exact. Standalone, against anchor 44be072. Independent of 0027-0029.

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS AT ALL. The temporal-filter SSE2 result (+1.20% A1 /
+0.34% A2 at exactly 0.00% BD-rate) makes a point this project had been
ignoring: a bit-exact speedup has an unbounded complexity-to-efficiency ratio
and cannot fail the bar at any preset. Eleven rounds went to approximation
patches that must clear 20-35. This is the first patch here in the other
category.

THE FINDING. A callgrind profile taken with screen-content tools disabled
attributes 2.77% of retired instructions to libc memset reached through
av2_rd_pick_intra_sby_mode. The source is one line:

    av2/encoder/intra_mode_search.c:1759   av2_zero(x->winner_mode_stats);
    av2/encoder/rdopt.c:9441               av2_zero(x->winner_mode_stats);

and the sizes are:

    WinnerModeStats           66,240 bytes
      MB_MODE_INFO               632
      RD_STATS                    40
      color_index_map         65,536     <- MAX_SB_SQUARE, 256x256
    array of 2, zeroed       132,480 bytes per call

**98.9% of the 132 KB cleared on every mode search is the palette colour map.**
A 4x4 luma block's intra mode search zeroes 128 KB of palette reconstruction
state before it starts.

WHY THE MAP DOES NOT NEED CLEARING. color_index_map is palette reconstruction
state. The palette search writes a winner's map at the moment it records that
winner, and nothing reads the map of a winner that was never recorded --
x->winner_mode_count bounds the valid entries and is reset to zero on the line
immediately after this call. The array is written before it is read.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES. Adds av2_reset_winner_mode_stats() in block.h, which
clears every field of WinnerModeStats except color_index_map, and calls it from
both sites. The fields are cleared one at a time rather than as a byte span, so
that adding a member to the struct produces a compile-time reminder rather than
a silently uninitialised field.

BIT-EXACTNESS, MEASURED. Six configurations, two clips, three QPs spanning the
CTC ladder, two presets -- every one byte-identical to the unpatched encoder:

    416x240 cpu-used=3 qp110   89ea7dc83488b29f == 89ea7dc83488b29f   BIT-EXACT
    416x240 cpu-used=3 qp185   8eb66b3a763fce0b == 8eb66b3a763fce0b   BIT-EXACT
    416x240 cpu-used=3 qp235   fb12fe15646b5730 == fb12fe15646b5730   BIT-EXACT
    192x128 cpu-used=4 qp110   5f1d203279c2905b == 5f1d203279c2905b   BIT-EXACT
    192x128 cpu-used=4 qp185   520ccf425745a016 == 520ccf425745a016   BIT-EXACT
    192x128 cpu-used=4 qp235   1e6fe90eef667bd6 == 1e6fe90eef667bd6   BIT-EXACT

This is evidence, not proof: it shows the map is written before it is read on
these paths, not on all paths. A CTC run reporting 0.00% BD-rate on every
metric -- which is what the temporal-filter patch got -- would settle it, and
that is the check to insist on.

THE SPEEDUP IS NOT YET MEASURED, AND I AM NOT GOING TO GUESS IT. Wall-clock
paired timing on this machine gave +1.09% (sd 3.89, n=3) and -0.79% (sd 4.30,
n=2) on two configurations -- a noise floor around 4%, far above anything this
change could produce. By this project's own rule, that is not a small speedup,
it is no measurement at all. Deterministic instruction counts are running; the
figure will follow. Do not quote a wall-clock number for this patch.

What can be said without measuring: the change removes 131,072 bytes of memory
traffic per call at two call sites, both of which fire once per mode search per
block. The upper bound on the gain is the profile line it targets.

WHAT TO RUN. One arm at any preset -- the change is preset-independent, and the
question it answers ("is it still 0.00% BD-rate at CTC scale?") is the same at
every preset. Speed 4 is the cheapest place to ask it.

RELATED WORK NOT IN THIS PATCH. The same profile shows a second large memset,
3.18% of instructions, inside av2_build_inter_predictors:

    if (plane == AVM_PLANE_Y)
      memset(xd->mv_refined, 0, 2 * N_OF_OFFSETS * sizeof(int_mv));

With OF_BSIZE_LOG2 == 3 and MAX_SB_SIZE_LOG2 == 8 that is 8,192 bytes cleared
on every luma inter-predictor build, for a block that may need two entries.
av2_get_optflow_based_mv() indexes mv_refined densely from zero over
n_blocks = (bw/n)*(bh/n) sub-blocks, so a bounded clear looks correct -- but n
can be 4 rather than OF_BSIZE when use_4x4 is set, and I have not established
the resulting worst-case bound. That check has to be done before the change is
safe, so it is written down here rather than shipped half-verified.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/block.h b/av2/encoder/block.h
index 2c76bf7..a3c5ff2 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/block.h
+++ b/av2/encoder/block.h
@@ -1776,6 +1776,43 @@ static AVM_INLINE bool is_bsize_gt(BLOCK_SIZE bsize1, BLOCK_SIZE bsize2) {
          block_size_high[bsize1] > block_size_high[bsize2];
 }
 
+/*!\brief Reset the winner-mode bookkeeping without clearing the palette map.
+ *
+ * `av2_zero(x->winner_mode_stats)` clears 132,480 bytes, of which 131,072
+ * (98.9%) is the two `color_index_map[MAX_SB_SQUARE]` arrays. Those arrays are
+ * palette reconstruction state: the palette search writes a winner's map when
+ * it records that winner, and nothing reads a map for a winner that was never
+ * recorded (`x->winner_mode_count` bounds the valid entries, and it is reset to
+ * zero alongside this call).
+ *
+ * Zeroing them therefore costs 128 KB of memory traffic per mode search, at
+ * both call sites, to initialise storage that is written before it is read. A
+ * callgrind profile with screen-content tools disabled attributes 2.77% of
+ * retired instructions to libc memset reached through
+ * `av2_rd_pick_intra_sby_mode` alone; the inter call site fires far more often
+ * in a random-access encode.
+ *
+ * Every other field is still cleared, field by field rather than by a span, so
+ * that adding a member to WinnerModeStats produces a compile-time reminder
+ * rather than a silently uninitialised field.
+ */
+static INLINE void av2_reset_winner_mode_stats(MACROBLOCK *x) {
+  const int n =
+      (int)(sizeof(x->winner_mode_stats) / sizeof(x->winner_mode_stats[0]));
+  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
+    WinnerModeStats *const ws = &x->winner_mode_stats[i];
+    av2_zero(ws->mbmi);
+    av2_zero(ws->rd_cost);
+    ws->rd = 0;
+    ws->rate_y = 0;
+    ws->rate_uv = 0;
+    ws->mode = 0;
+    ws->refs[0] = 0;
+    ws->refs[1] = 0;
+    // ws->color_index_map is deliberately NOT cleared -- see above.
+  }
+}
+
 static INLINE void set_blk_skip(uint8_t txb_skip[], int blk_idx, int skip) {
   if (skip)
     txb_skip[blk_idx] |= 1UL;
